add('ind_2023_182213',4.5,'Partitioning by squarefree part turns the ordered-pair count into a sum of squares; the obstruction modulo 8 then forces four classes.',r'''Put two elements in the same class when their squarefree parts agree. Their product is a square exactly when they lie in one class, so if the class sizes are $a_1,\ldots,a_t$, the hypothesis says $\sum a_i^2=2023$. If $t\le3$, pad by zeroes to express $2023$ as three squares. This is impossible modulo $8$, because squares are $0,1,4$ and three such residues cannot sum to $7$. Thus there are at least four classes; one representative from each gives the required elements.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Squares & powers','Modular arithmetic'],note='Replaced the incomplete trial-enumeration argument in the transcription with a complete modular proof.')
add('ind_2023_a65b08',4.5,'Reversing the polynomial exposes its three low coefficients, and choosing the smallest coefficient makes Vieta’s sum-of-squares inequality impossible.',r'''Choose $k$ so that $a_{k+1}$ is the smallest of the $101$ numbers. If the corresponding polynomial has real roots, its reciprocal polynomial $Q(x)=x^{100}P_k(1/x)$ also has $100$ real roots $r_1,\ldots,r_{100}$. Its leading three coefficients are $a_k,a_{k+1},a_{k+2}$, so Vieta gives
$$\sum r_i=-\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k},\qquad \sum_{i<j}r_ir_j=\frac{a_{k+2}}{a_k}.$$
Consequently $a_{k+1}^2=a_k^2\sum r_i^2+2a_ka_{k+2}\ge2a_ka_{k+2}$. But minimality gives $a_{k+1}^2\le a_ka_{k+2}$, impossible since all coefficients are positive.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Polynomials','Extremal arguments','Algebraic identities'],note='The transcription incorrectly assumes sums of real-rooted polynomials are real-rooted; replaced with an independent Vieta proof.')
add('ind_2023_aa198e',6.5,'A density bound rules out slopes below one half; subtracting an integer slope and reflecting the function force the fractional part of the slope.',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Functional equations','Floors & fractional parts','Pigeonhole principle'])
add('ind_2023_616c71',5.5,'A pairing strategy supplies the sharp upper bound, while a local three-move construction creates adjacent tails that permit an independent alternating completion.',r'''The winning values are $1,2,\ldots,N+1$. Hobbes can pair $N$ disjoint adjacent pairs and leave one coin unpaired. Whenever Calvin creates two tails in a pair, Hobbes changes the old tail back. Thus at most $N+1$ tails survive each reply.

For the converse, Calvin first turns coins $1$, $3$, then $2$. Hobbes cannot undo either of the first two moves. After the third move there are adjacent tails, either $1,2$ or $2,3$; if Hobbes declines, choose $1,2$ and regard coin $3$ as an additional tail. Reserve the adjacent pair and their two exterior neighbours. The remaining $2N-3$ coins form a path, all heads, on which Calvin turns every other coin, beginning and ending at the path endpoints. These $N-1$ chosen positions are adjacent neither to one another nor to the reserved tails, so Hobbes cannot undo them. The final count is at least $N+1$. The number of tails after a reply never decreases and rises by at most one, so every smaller target is attained along the way.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Games & strategies','Invariants','Algorithms & constructions'])
# If Hobbes declined after move 3, the exterior neighbour 3 is tails; select the other adjacent pair to leave it exterior. A direct alternative avoids this detail.
r['ind_2023_616c71']['solutionOverride']=r'''The winning values are $1,2,\ldots,N+1$. Hobbes pairs $N$ disjoint adjacent pairs, leaving one coin unpaired. Whenever Calvin creates two tails in a pair, Hobbes changes the old tail back. Hence at most $N+1$ tails survive a reply.

Calvin can attain $N$ tails by turning coins $1,3,\ldots,2N-1$, numbered cyclically; Hobbes cannot respond. Calvin next turns coin $2$. If Hobbes declines, there are already $N+1$ tails. If Hobbes flips coin $1$, Calvin turns $2N+1$, which has two heads as neighbours, and wins. Otherwise Hobbes flips coin $3$. Calvin successively turns $4,6,\ldots,2N-2$. On turning $2j$, its left neighbour $2j-1$ is heads and its right neighbour $2j+1$ is tails; to avoid an immediate $N+1$ tails, Hobbes must flip the latter. Finally Calvin turns $2N$, whose neighbours $2N-1$ and $2N+1$ are now both heads, and wins. Since the surviving tail count increases only by zero or one per turn, every smaller target is also reached.'''
add('ind_2023_593616',6,'Separates distance exponents by parity and turns each set into a forest: a geometric-series estimate forbids a cycle with a uniquely longest-scale edge.',r'''For each distinct even exponent that occurs, choose one pair of points with that exponent and draw an edge. If these selected edges contained a cycle, let its largest exponent be $2m$. That edge has length at least $2^{2m}$. All other edges have distinct smaller even exponents, so the sum of their lengths is less than
$$\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}2^{2(m-j)+1}=\frac23\,2^{2m},$$
contradicting the polygonal triangle inequality. Thus the chosen edges form a forest and number at most $n-1$. Exactly the same argument applies to odd exponents. The total is at most $2n-2<2n$ (and for $n=1$ no values occur).''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Graph theory','Combinatorial geometry','Extremal arguments'],note='Replaced an invalid diameter/minimum-separation bound in the transcription with a complete forest argument.')
