add('ind_2024_0502bc',4.5,'Finds the midpoint of a constructed chord using the isosceles hypothesis, then translates the circumcentre condition into a cyclic-angle equality.',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Triangle centres','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals','Similarity & congruence'],numberOverride='1')
add('ind_2024_36beb1',4,'Tracks the number of contaminated rows and columns: the first move has an extra cost, and a rectangular construction attains the resulting bound.',r'''Let $n=2024$ and $t=1000$. Call a row or column used if it contains a red cell. The first move creates $t+1$ used lines, and every further move creates at least one new used line. There are only $2n$ lines, so at most $2n-t$ moves can occur and at most $t(2n-t)=3\,048\,000$ cells become red. To attain this, process all $n$ columns, colouring their top $t$ cells. Then process each of the remaining $n-t$ wholly white rows, colouring any $t$ cells. This makes exactly $2n-t$ legal moves.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Monovariants','Extremal arguments','Algorithms & constructions'],numberOverride='2')
add('ind_2024_5be055',4.5,'Raises all three congruences to a common exponent; the parity of the complementary exponents produces a sign contradiction around the cycle.',r'''Put $L=2023\cdot2024\cdot2025$. Raising the first congruence to the even power $2024\cdot2025$ gives $a^L\equiv b^L\pmod p$. Raising the second to the odd power $2023\cdot2025$ gives $b^L\equiv-c^L$, and raising the third to the even power $2023\cdot2024$ gives $c^L\equiv a^L$. Therefore $2a^L\equiv0\pmod p$. Since $p$ is an odd prime, $p\mid a$; the first and second original congruences then force $p\mid b,c$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Modular arithmetic','Parity'],numberOverride='3')
add('ind_2024_29557d',6,'Splits by finite or infinite image, constructing specially sized sets in each case; the finite-image case needs a fibre argument before the final restriction.',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Functional equations','Pigeonhole principle'],numberOverride='4')
add('ind_2024_989122',6.5,'Recognises each three-rotation composition as a half-turn centred at an incircle contact point, so the desired circumradius reduces to Euler’s inequality.',r'''Let $D_i$ be the incircle contact point on $A_{i+1}A_{i+2}$, and let its radius be $r$. Equality of tangent lengths and the counter-clockwise orientation show that the successive rotations in the definition send $D_i$ to $D_{i+2}$, then to $D_{i+1}$, and back to $D_i$. Their total angle is $180^\circ$, so their composition is the half-turn about $D_i$. Thus, in vector form, $P_i=2D_i-P$. The triangle $P_1P_2P_3$ is therefore a translation of a scale-two copy of the contact triangle, whose circumradius is $r$. Its circumradius is exactly $2r$. Euler's inequality $2r\le R$ for the original triangle proves the result.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Transformations','Triangle centres','Geometric inequalities'],numberOverride='5')
add('ind_2024_3d5780',5,'Rationalises two related radical sums to place a half-integer between them and bound their separation; parity then determines their floors.',r'''Put $M=n^2+n/2$. Since every radical lies strictly between $n$ and $n+1$,
$$A_n-n^2=\sum_{j=1}^n\frac{2j-1}{\sqrt{n^2+2j-1}+n}<\frac{n^2}{2n}=\frac n2,$$
whereas
$$B_n-n^2>\sum_{j=1}^n\frac{2j}{2n+1}=\frac{n(n+1)}{2n+1}>\frac n2.$$
Also $0<B_n-A_n<\sum_{j=1}^n1/(2n)=1/2$. Thus $A_n<M<B_n$ and both numbers lie within $1/2$ of $M$. If $n$ is even, the integer $M$ separates their floors. If $n$ is odd, both lie strictly between the consecutive integers $M-1/2$ and $M+1/2$, so their floors agree. Exactly the odd $n\ge3$ work.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Floors & fractional parts','Inequalities','Algebraic identities'],numberOverride='6')
